---
title: 'The New GetHired for Companies: What It Means for Job Seekers'
publishedAt: 2026-10-21
type: job-seekers
---
**Quick answer:** Companies can now hire directly on GetHired, which brings job seekers three new things. Recruiters can find your public profile and send you a message, which you answer in your Opportunities inbox, and your contact details stay private until you say you're interested. You can see how many companies viewed your profile. And some jobs include a short AI screening interview after you apply. This guide explains each one and how to make the most of it.

Until now, GetHired was mostly a place to search for jobs, check your resume and practise interviews. With [GetHired for Companies](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/introducing-gethired-for-companies-an-ai-recruiter-for-startup-tech-hiring), startups and tech teams now post roles, review applicants and reach out to candidates on the platform. Here's what changes for you.

## 1. Companies can come to you

When a company posts a job, GetHired matches it against candidates with public profiles. If you're a good fit, a recruiter may send you a message: an invite to apply, or a note asking whether you're open to a chat.

### Your Opportunities inbox

You'll get an email, but you answer on GetHired, on your [Opportunities](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/opportunities) page. Each message shows the company, the job and why you were matched. You can reply:

- **Interested**, with an optional note and how you'd like to be contacted;
- **Not interested**, with an optional reason, such as not looking right now, location or compensation.

If the message includes a job, saying Interested lets you apply in one click.

### Your contact details stay private

Companies browsing the talent pool see your skills, experience, desired roles, preferred locations, salary range and resume content, but not your email, phone number, LinkedIn, GitHub or original resume file. Those are shared with a company only when you **apply** to one of its jobs or reply **Interested** to its message. This is enforced by GetHired's database, not just hidden on the page.

You stay in control:

- Turn off outreach in your settings if you want to be discoverable but not contacted.
- Block a company and it won't see your profile at all.
- Report a message that's inappropriate.
- Companies can contact you at most three times in 90 days, and messages expire after 30 days.

### How to get found

1. **Make your profile public** and keep it current.
2. **Fill in desired roles, skills and preferred locations.** Matching uses them directly.
3. **Set a realistic salary range.** It helps companies know you're in their budget.
4. **Write career goals in plain language.** "I want to move from backend to platform engineering" is useful.
5. **Keep a strong primary resume.** Our [ATS resume checklist](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-check-if-your-resume-will-pass-an-ats-in-2026-and-fix-it-in-5-minutes) applies here too.

## 2. See who's looking

Your dashboard now shows how many companies viewed your profile in the last 30 days. Company names appear only for companies that have messaged you. If the count is zero for a while, it's a sign to update your profile or skills.

## 3. AI screening interviews

Some companies add a short AI voice interview to their jobs. It's different from GetHired's practice interviews:

{% table %}
-
- Practice interview
- Screening interview
---
- Who starts it
- You, for any job
- The company invites you after you apply
---
- Attempts
- As many as you like
- One, plus one retry if something goes wrong technically
---
- Who sees the result
- Only you
- The company sees a report
---
- Feedback
- Coaching tips for you
- Scores and evidence for the company
---
- Cost to you
- Uses credits
- Free
{% /table %}

### How it works

1. **Apply first.** Your application is saved before the interview, so it's never lost.
2. **Get invited.** If the company invites you, you'll see it right after applying, by email and on your dashboard. You can start straight away or do it later before the deadline.
3. **Take the interview** in your browser with a microphone. Expect around 10 to 20 minutes of questions about your experience.
4. **A person decides.** The company reads a report with what you said. Nobody is rejected by the AI.

### How to prepare

- **Practise first.** Use a [GetHired practice interview](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/ai-interview) for the same job to warm up, and read our tips on [remote interviews](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/the-art-of-the-remote-interview-from-preparation-to-follow-up). Our [25 software engineer interview questions](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/top-25-software-engineer-interview-questions-and-how-to-answer-them) is a good list to rehearse.
- **Find a quiet place** and check your microphone and connection.
- **Use real examples.** The report is built from quotes of what you said, so specific stories ("I cut our API latency from 800 to 120 ms by…") count for much more than general claims.
- **Answer the question asked**, then stop. The interviewer will follow up if it needs more.
- **It's okay to pause and think.**

## 4. Share your best work with an application

When you apply to a job posted on GetHired, you can attach a completed **AI mock interview** or **resume review** to your application. The company sees a summary written for employers (competency scores, evidence and suggested follow-up questions), labelled as shared by you. Your transcript is never shared, and you can remove the attachment until the company opens it.

It's a simple way to stand out in a pile of resumes.

## 5. Better applications on careers pages

Companies now have branded careers pages on GetHired, at addresses like gethired.devhub.co.in/careers/company-name, and their own application forms. Some questions are optional and some include help text explaining what the company wants. Answer required questions carefully. Short or generic answers are easy to spot.

## What hasn't changed

- Searching and applying to jobs from across the web works the same as before. Our guide to [applying on Greenhouse, Workday, Lever and Workable](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-apply-to-jobs-on-greenhouse-workday-lever-and-workable-a-step-by-step-guide) still applies to those.
- Your practice interviews stay private. Companies see one only if you attach it to an application, and even then they get a summary, not the transcript.
- GetHired's AI never sees your name, photo, age, gender or other protected characteristics when it compares you to a job. We explain how in [fair AI hiring on GetHired](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/fair-ai-hiring-how-gethired-keeps-bias-out-of-candidate-screening).

Update your profile on your [dashboard](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/dashboard) and make it public to start getting found.
